WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.294
Four photos of a living room go in.

00:00:02.674 --> 00:00:05.936
And out comes a walk through the room, along a path you choose.

00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:08.947
There is no point cloud, no mesh and no splats behind it.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:12.511
Hi, I'm Homeward!

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:13.312
This is AVM.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:16.517
It keeps a scene as a pile of views, and looks them

00:00:16.517 --> 00:00:18.120
up by where the camera stands.

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:21.279
A splat fits geometry once.

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:24.954
Every render agrees, but it knows nothing you did not photograph.

00:00:25.374 --> 00:00:28.990
AVM keeps the views themselves, and generates new ones from them.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.291
It can invent what the photos missed.

00:00:32.758 --> 00:00:36.070
Every view, the photos you give it and the frames it makes,

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.554
goes into one pool, tagged with its camera pose.

00:00:38.974 --> 00:00:42.323
Before each chunk, it fetches the few old views whose cameras could

00:00:42.323 --> 00:00:44.276
see what the next camera will see.

00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:48.195
Add the prefill sink and the last few frames, and the transformer

00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:49.653
generates the next short clip.

00:00:50.073 --> 00:00:51.585
Those frames join the pool.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:52.795
The pool grows forever.

00:00:52.795 --> 00:00:54.912
What the model attends to stays small.

00:00:56.908 --> 00:00:59.012
Here is that in a toy, from above.

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:02.168
The camera slides along a room, out and back, and every view

00:01:02.168 --> 00:01:04.272
lands in the pool where it was taken.

00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:08.180
On the way back, the motion window holds the last few frames.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.249
They are the model's own recent output, so any error in them carries forward.

00:01:12.669 --> 00:01:13.707
So fetch by pose.

00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:16.823
The views taken at this spot on the way out see the

00:01:16.823 --> 00:01:18.900
same wall, and so does the original photo.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.015
Now the model can copy the room as it first saw it.

00:01:23.658 --> 00:01:26.994
This is a real rollout from one image: the camera pans away

00:01:26.994 --> 00:01:28.106
round a living room.

00:01:28.486 --> 00:01:31.023
And it lands back on the same armchairs and cushions.

00:01:33.008 --> 00:01:36.715
The paper's ablation says it is the lookup doing the work.

00:01:36.715 --> 00:01:40.759
Revisit PSNR is twenty-eight with retrieval, and eleven without it, level with

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:42.107
the best world model.

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:47.917
Underneath is Wan, a fourteen billion parameter image to video model.

00:01:48.317 --> 00:01:52.546
It is fine-tuned to take many context slots, images, clips or other

00:01:52.546 --> 00:01:54.661
cameras, and produce one target clip.

00:01:55.061 --> 00:01:58.725
Then it is made causal, block by block, so finished frames can be cached.

00:01:59.125 --> 00:02:02.570
And it is distilled on its own long rollouts, with the same

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.579
retrieved context it will see at inference.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:06.778
Fifty sampling steps become four.

00:02:08.758 --> 00:02:13.251
The paper's comparisons: from a few posed photos, against a view synthesis model.

00:02:13.671 --> 00:02:17.111
And on a revisit, where a world model without retrieval comes back

00:02:17.111 --> 00:02:18.258
to a different room.

00:02:19.608 --> 00:02:22.233
It follows text prompts worse than the world models do.

00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:24.877
It moves the camera well and the world less.

00:02:25.807 --> 00:02:28.783
And there is no speed figure, no code and no weights yet.

00:02:30.058 --> 00:02:32.150
The idea to keep is the index.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:35.737
File every view by pose, fetch by what the next camera will

00:02:35.737 --> 00:02:38.726
see, and the attention stays small however long you walk.

00:02:40.108 --> 00:02:43.036
A pool of posed views, fetched by coverage, and a room that

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:44.744
is still there when you come back.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:47.257
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:47.257 --> 00:02:47.855
I'm Homeward.

00:02:47.855 --> 00:02:48.154
Bye!
